//? Title: Partial and semipartial correlations
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Measure the association between two variables after removing the linear influence of others, attach tests and intervals to it, and see when adjusting removes a spurious correlation and when it creates one

The **partial correlation** \(r_{xy\cdot Z}\) is the correlation between the parts of \(x\) and \(y\) that are not linearly predictable from covariates \(Z\): the residuals of the regressions of \(x\) and of \(y\) on \(Z\). For a single covariate,

\[r_{xy\cdot z}=\frac{r_{xy}-r_{xz}r_{yz}}{\sqrt{(1-r_{xz}^2)(1-r_{yz}^2)}},\]

and in general it equals \(-P_{xy}/\sqrt{P_{xx}P_{yy}}\) for the inverse \(P\) of the covariance matrix. The **semipartial (part) correlation** \(r_{x(y\cdot Z)}\) removes \(Z\) from \(y\) only. Its square is the increase in \(R^2\) when \(y\) is added to a regression of \(x\) on \(Z\): the unique contribution of \(y\).

The partial correlation is tested with \(t=r\sqrt{(n-2-k)/(1-r^2)}\) on \(n-2-k\) degrees of freedom. This is the t test of \(y\)'s coefficient in the regression of \(x\) on \(Z\) and \(y\), and so it is also the correct test that the semipartial correlation is zero. `ppcor::spcor.test` plugs the semipartial correlation into the same formula, which understates \(t\) whenever \(Z\) explains part of \(x\). The library reports the coefficient test for both. The Fisher interval \(\tanh(\operatorname{atanh}r\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}/\sqrt{n-3-k})\) applies to the partial correlation under multivariate normality. The semipartial correlation has no such interval, so use the bootstrap.

`partial_correlation` and `semipartial_correlation` work from data. Each uses Pearson correlation, or Spearman correlation on ranks, and returns the t test, the Fisher interval and an optional percentile bootstrap interval. `partial_correlation_matrix` gives all pairwise partial and semipartial correlations given the remaining variables, from a covariance or correlation matrix and its sample size. The tests reproduce `ppcor::pcor.test` and `pcor` (estimates, statistics and p-values, Pearson and Spearman) and the `spcor.test`/`spcor` estimates to \(10^{-12}\). They check the semipartial test against the coefficient t test of `lm`, and the data and matrix forms against each other.

The example asks how fuel consumption (mpg) relates to engine power (hp) in `mtcars` once weight is accounted for. The simulation (2000 samples of n = 100 each) has two parts:

- Three causal structures in which \(x\) and \(y\) have no direct link: a confounder \(z\) measured exactly, the same confounder measured with reliability 0.6, and a collider \(z=x+y+e\).
- The coverage of Fisher and bootstrap intervals for a true partial correlation of 0.3 given three covariates, under normal and heavy-tailed (multivariate \(t_3\)) data.

```{cpp, id=pc, file="figures/partial_correlation.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/datasets/datasets.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/partial_correlation.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::PartialMethod;

std::string interval(double lo, double hi) {
    return std::isnan(lo) ? std::string("—") : std::format("[{:.3f}, {:.3f}]", lo, hi);
}

double chi_square_draw(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, int df) {
    double s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < df; ++i) {
        const double z = rng.normal();
        s += z * z;
    }
    return s;
}

int main() {
    const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
    auto col = [&](const char* name) { return cars.get_column<double>(name).values(); };
    const auto mpg = col("mpg"), hp = col("hp"), wt = col("wt"), disp = col("disp");
    const auto qsec = col("qsec");
    std::println("| Association of mpg with hp | Method | Estimate | t (df) | p | "
                 "Fisher 95% CI | Bootstrap 95% CI |");
    std::println("| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto method : {PartialMethod::pearson, PartialMethod::spearman}) {
        const char* m = method == PartialMethod::pearson ? "Pearson" : "Spearman";
        const dm::stats::PartialOptions o{.method = method, .bootstrap = 2000, .seed = 5};
        const auto raw = dm::stats::partial_correlation(mpg, hp, {}, o);
        const auto par = dm::stats::partial_correlation(mpg, hp, {wt}, o);
        const auto semi = dm::stats::semipartial_correlation(mpg, hp, {wt}, o);
        for (const auto& [name, r] : {std::pair{"Raw", raw}, {"Partial, given wt", par},
                                      {"Semipartial (hp adjusted for wt)", semi}})
            std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} ({}) | {:.4f} | {} | {} |", name, m,
                         r.estimate, r.statistic, r.df, r.p_value,
                         interval(r.lower, r.upper),
                         interval(r.bootstrap_lower, r.bootstrap_upper));
    }
    const auto semi = dm::stats::semipartial_correlation(mpg, hp, {wt});
    std::println("\nSquared semipartial correlation (R^2 gained by adding hp to mpg ~ wt): "
                 "{:.3f}.\n", semi.estimate * semi.estimate);

    // Partial correlation matrix of five variables, each pair given the other three.
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>> vars{mpg, hp, wt, disp, qsec};
    const char* names[5] = {"mpg", "hp", "wt", "disp", "qsec"};
    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> cov(5, 5, 0.0);
    const double n = static_cast<double>(mpg.size());
    std::vector<double> mean(5, 0.0);
    for (int a = 0; a < 5; ++a)
        for (const double v : vars[a]) mean[a] += v / n;
    for (int a = 0; a < 5; ++a)
        for (int b = 0; b < 5; ++b)
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < mpg.size(); ++i)
                cov(a, b) += (vars[a][i] - mean[a]) * (vars[b][i] - mean[b]) / (n - 1);
    const auto pm = dm::stats::partial_correlation_matrix(cov, mpg.size());
    std::println("| Partial r (p) | mpg | hp | wt | disp | qsec |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (int a = 0; a < 5; ++a) {
        std::string row = std::format("| {} |", names[a]);
        for (int b = 0; b < 5; ++b)
            row += a == b ? " — |"
                          : std::format(" {:.2f} ({:.3f}) |", pm.partial(a, b),
                                        pm.partial_p_value(a, b));
        std::println("{}", row);
    }

    // Simulation 1: causal structures without a direct x-y link.
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(17);
    const int reps = 2000;
    const std::size_t m = 100;
    std::println("\n| Structure | Mean raw r | Raw test rejects | Mean partial r | "
                 "Partial test rejects |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const char* structures[3] = {"Confounder z measured exactly",
                                 "Confounder measured with reliability 0.6",
                                 "Collider z = x + y + e"};
    for (int s = 0; s < 3; ++s) {
        double raw = 0, raw_rej = 0, par = 0, par_rej = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            std::vector<double> x(m), y(m), z(m);
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
                if (s < 2) {
                    const double c = rng.normal();
                    x[i] = 0.7 * c + rng.normal() * std::sqrt(0.51);
                    y[i] = 0.7 * c + rng.normal() * std::sqrt(0.51);
                    // Observed confounder: exact, or error variance 2/3 (reliability 0.6).
                    z[i] = s == 0 ? c : c + std::sqrt(2.0 / 3) * rng.normal();
                } else {
                    x[i] = rng.normal(), y[i] = rng.normal();
                    z[i] = x[i] + y[i] + 0.5 * rng.normal();
                }
            }
            const auto a = dm::stats::partial_correlation(x, y, {});
            const auto b = dm::stats::partial_correlation(x, y, {z});
            raw += a.estimate / reps, raw_rej += (a.p_value < 0.05) / double(reps);
            par += b.estimate / reps, par_rej += (b.p_value < 0.05) / double(reps);
        }
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", structures[s], raw,
                     raw_rej, par, par_rej);
    }

    // Simulation 2: coverage for a true partial correlation of 0.3 given three covariates.
    std::println("\n| Data | n | Fisher-z coverage | Bootstrap coverage | "
                 "Mean Fisher width | Mean bootstrap width |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const bool heavy : {false, true})
        for (const std::size_t size : {30uz, 100uz}) {
            double fc = 0, bc = 0, fw = 0, bw = 0;
            const int sims = 1000;
            for (int r = 0; r < sims; ++r) {
                std::vector<double> x(size), y(size);
                std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(3, std::vector<double>(size));
                for (std::size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
                    const double w = heavy ? std::sqrt(3 / chi_square_draw(rng, 3)) : 1.0;
                    double zx = 0, zy = 0;
                    for (int c = 0; c < 3; ++c) {
                        z[c][i] = w * rng.normal();
                        zx += 0.5 * z[c][i], zy += (c - 1) * 0.6 * z[c][i];
                    }
                    const double e1 = rng.normal();
                    const double e2 = 0.3 * e1 + std::sqrt(0.91) * rng.normal();
                    x[i] = zx + w * e1, y[i] = zy + w * e2;
                }
                const auto p = dm::stats::partial_correlation(
                    x, y, z, {.bootstrap = 300, .seed = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(r + 1)});
                fc += p.lower <= 0.3 && 0.3 <= p.upper;
                bc += p.bootstrap_lower <= 0.3 && 0.3 <= p.bootstrap_upper;
                fw += (p.upper - p.lower) / sims;
                bw += (p.bootstrap_upper - p.bootstrap_lower) / sims;
            }
            std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                         heavy ? "Multivariate t3" : "Normal", size, fc / sims, bc / sims,
                         fw, bw);
        }

    // Figure: 400 units from the collider structure; highlight those with |z| < 0.5.
    std::vector<double> ax, ay, sx, sy;
    for (int i = 0; i < 400; ++i) {
        const double x = rng.normal(), y = rng.normal(), z = x + y + 0.5 * rng.normal();
        ax.push_back(x), ay.push_back(y);
        if (std::abs(z) < 0.5) sx.push_back(x), sy.push_back(y);
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.points(ax, ay, "All units", {200, 200, 200}, 5)
        .points(sx, sy, "Units with |z| < 0.5", {26, 90, 147}, 6)
        .y_limits(-3.5, 4.5)
        .title("Conditioning on a collider z = x + y + e")
        .x_label("x")
        .y_label("y")
        .size(620, 430);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/partial_correlation.svg");
}
```
