\image(figures/partial_correlation.svg)
// result_end

Heavier cars have more powerful engines, so the raw correlation of −0.78 between mpg and horsepower mixes the two. Given weight, the partial correlation is −0.55: among cars of equal weight, more power still means lower mileage. The semipartial correlation (−0.27) answers a different question. Horsepower's weight-free part adds 7.4% to the \(R^2\) of mpg on weight. That is the correct size measure for "unique contribution", and its test (t = −3.52, p = 0.0015) is the same as the partial correlation's. `ppcor::spcor.test` reports p = 0.14 for the same quantity, because it plugs the semipartial correlation into a formula that ignores the variance weight already explains.

Spearman versions tell the same story with somewhat larger magnitudes. In the five-variable matrix, the partial correlation of mpg with hp shrinks to −0.22 (p = 0.24) once displacement and quarter-mile time are also held fixed. Those two absorb much of what power measures, and weight is the only variable left with a clear direct association with mpg. Each partial correlation is conditional on the specific set of covariates, and its meaning changes as that set changes.

The first simulation shows when adjustment helps and when it harms. With the confounder measured exactly, the partial correlation is centred on zero and the test holds its 5% size. With the confounder measured at reliability 0.6, more than half the spurious association remains (0.28), and the test "finds" a direct link 80% of the time. Adjusting for a noisy proxy only partly adjusts. Conditioning on a collider creates a strong negative association (−0.80) between two independent variables, as the figure shows: among units with the sum held near zero, a large x must come with a small y. The causal structure, not the data, decides what to adjust for.

For a true partial correlation of 0.3 with normal data, the Fisher interval covers at the nominal rate even at n = 30. Under multivariate \(t_3\) data, its coverage falls to 82% at n = 30 and 70% at n = 100. The error does not shrink with more data, because the variance of the estimator depends on fourth moments. The percentile bootstrap holds 91–93%, with intervals about 45% wider at n = 100.

>> Practical guidance

- Report the partial correlation for "association holding Z fixed" and the squared semipartial correlation for "unique variance explained". Test both with the regression coefficient t test, which is what this library reports.
- Choose covariates from a causal argument. Adjust for confounders; never adjust for colliders or for consequences of x or y. Measurement error in a confounder leaves residual confounding, so a nonzero partial correlation is not proof of a direct link.
- Use Fisher intervals only for roughly normal data. With heavy tails or outliers, use the bootstrap or Spearman partial correlations.
- Partial correlations measure linear (or, with ranks, monotone) conditional association. Zero partial correlation is conditional independence only under joint normality.
- With many variables and a modest n, estimate the precision matrix with regularization, as in the graphical lasso, instead of inverting the sample covariance.

>> References

- R. A. Fisher (1924). The distribution of the partial correlation coefficient. *Metron* 3, 329-332.
- S. Kim (2015). ppcor: An R package for a fast calculation to semi-partial correlation coefficients. *Communications for Statistical Applications and Methods* 22(6), 665-674. doi:10.5351/CSAM.2015.22.6.665
- J. Cohen, P. Cohen, S. G. West and L. S. Aiken (2003). *Applied Multiple Regression/Correlation Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences*, 3rd ed., ch. 3. Erlbaum.
- J. Pearl (2009). *Causality*, 2nd ed., ch. 3. Cambridge University Press.
- K. A. Bollen (1989). *Structural Equations with Latent Variables*, ch. 5 (measurement error in covariates). Wiley.
